From: wangzlei <66336933+wangzlei@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 19:52:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 10/11] kube-service-cidrs: allow Kubernetes Service addresses
 as tcp_service_name peers

A client connected to a Kubernetes Service sees the ClusterIP as its peer
(kube-proxy DNATs to the pod later), and the Service CIDR is usually outside
the VPC (EKS: 10.100.0.0/16 or 172.20.0.0/16). The VPC-only peer filter
therefore never exchanged the service name on Service traffic, so client RED
had no peer.service.name on EKS (seen on the ebpf-mode EKS test cluster).

When the Kubernetes metadata decorator is enabled (or autodetected), the
tracer also allows the cluster's Service addresses:
- the networking.k8s.io/v1 ServiceCIDR objects (Kubernetes 1.33+), when the
  API serves them and the agent may list them;
- otherwise every Service's ClusterIPs, one /32 or /128 entry each.
Both are watched and kept current. Startup waits at most 10s for the initial
list before sockops is attached. The trie's max_entries is raised from 256 to
65536 at load time (no BPF change; the trie is not preallocated). EC2 / ECS
(no Kubernetes) are unchanged, and addresses outside the VPC and the cluster
are still never sent the service name.

Adot-Obi-Patch: 10-kube-service-cidrs
